WEBVTT

00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:01.730
Last time, we left a clue.

00:00:02.393 --> 00:00:05.241
Pass an electric current through hydrogen gas, and

00:00:05.282 --> 00:00:05.762
it glows.

00:00:06.425 --> 00:00:08.114
Now spread that light out with a prism,

00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:09.783
and you don't get a rainbow.

00:00:10.306 --> 00:00:12.589
You get just four sharp coloured lines:

00:00:13.151 --> 00:00:16.857
red, blue-green, blue and violet, with darkness in

00:00:16.878 --> 00:00:17.198
between.

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:19.419
Compare a hot lamp filament.

00:00:19.883 --> 00:00:21.949
Its light spreads into every colour, with no

00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:22.271
gaps.

00:00:23.034 --> 00:00:25.300
So why does hydrogen give out only these

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:26.103
few colours?

00:00:26.907 --> 00:00:29.154
The answer tells us how electrons are arranged

00:00:29.255 --> 00:00:29.756
in an atom.

00:00:30.757 --> 00:00:32.724
First, we need one idea about light.

00:00:33.468 --> 00:00:36.605
Light carries energy in tiny packets, called photons.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.118
Each photon's energy sets its colour.

00:00:39.762 --> 00:00:41.649
Red photons carry the least energy of the

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.662
visible colours, and violet photons carry the most.

00:00:45.366 --> 00:00:47.755
So a single sharp line means photons that

00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:49.985
all have the same, exact energy.

00:00:51.128 --> 00:00:53.073
Now, the nuclear model of the atom, from

00:00:53.275 --> 00:00:56.383
Rutherford's gold foil experiments, has a tiny nucleus

00:00:56.485 --> 00:00:57.567
with electrons around it.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.243
But nothing in that model limits an electron's

00:01:00.425 --> 00:01:00.726
energy.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.052
If an electron could have any energy at

00:01:03.174 --> 00:01:05.737
all, it could lose any amount, and the

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:07.921
atom could give out photons of every energy.

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:10.330
That would be a smooth rainbow, like the

00:01:10.351 --> 00:01:10.692
filament.

00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:13.791
Hydrogen's lines say that can't be right.

00:01:14.581 --> 00:01:17.692
In nineteen thirteen, Niels Bohr proposed a fix.

00:01:18.274 --> 00:01:20.842
An electron can only have certain fixed energies,

00:01:21.285 --> 00:01:22.387
called energy levels.

00:01:23.091 --> 00:01:24.660
Think of a staircase, not a ramp.

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:26.932
You can stand on any step, but never

00:01:26.992 --> 00:01:27.816
between steps.

00:01:28.558 --> 00:01:30.840
The lowest level, where an electron normally sits,

00:01:31.261 --> 00:01:32.463
is called the ground state.

00:01:33.249 --> 00:01:34.575
Here's how the light is made.

00:01:35.358 --> 00:01:37.822
The electric current, or a hot flame, gives

00:01:37.943 --> 00:01:40.046
an electron energy, and it jumps up to

00:01:40.087 --> 00:01:40.747
a higher level.

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:42.976
We say the atom is excited.

00:01:43.621 --> 00:01:44.628
But it doesn't stay there.

00:01:45.091 --> 00:01:46.993
The electron soon drops back to a lower

00:01:47.034 --> 00:01:49.298
level, and as it drops, it gives out

00:01:49.559 --> 00:01:50.239
one photon.

00:01:50.922 --> 00:01:53.614
That photon's energy is exactly the energy gap

00:01:53.715 --> 00:01:54.839
between the two levels.

00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:57.608
And an electron sitting in a level gives

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:58.652
out no light at all.

00:01:59.237 --> 00:02:00.809
Light only comes from a jump.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:02.931
Now the lines make sense.

00:02:03.536 --> 00:02:06.021
The levels are fixed, so the gaps between

00:02:06.062 --> 00:02:08.508
them are fixed, and each gap makes photons

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:11.395
of one exact energy, and one exact colour.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:14.560
For hydrogen, the four visible lines come from

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.364
electrons dropping down to the second level, from

00:02:17.405 --> 00:02:20.268
the third, fourth, fifth and sixth levels.

00:02:21.131 --> 00:02:23.635
A bigger drop releases more energy, so its

00:02:23.717 --> 00:02:25.540
line sits further toward violet.

00:02:26.203 --> 00:02:27.827
Drops all the way to the lowest level

00:02:27.929 --> 00:02:31.138
release even more energy, as ultraviolet light, which

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.122
our eyes can't see.

00:02:32.774 --> 00:02:33.367
Quick prediction.

00:02:33.891 --> 00:02:36.222
Here's an atom with just three energy levels.

00:02:36.826 --> 00:02:39.089
When its electrons drop down, how many different

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.273
lines could its emission spectrum show?

00:02:41.977 --> 00:02:42.360
Have a think.

00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:46.920
Three.

00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:49.507
An electron can drop from the top level

00:02:49.609 --> 00:02:51.311
to the middle, from the middle to the

00:02:51.352 --> 00:02:53.235
bottom, or straight from the top to the

00:02:53.276 --> 00:02:53.516
bottom.

00:02:54.178 --> 00:02:56.303
Each drop has its own gap, so each

00:02:56.364 --> 00:02:57.346
makes its own line.

00:02:57.909 --> 00:02:59.793
The straight drop is the biggest, so its

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:01.358
photon has the most energy.

00:03:02.275 --> 00:03:04.360
This is why line spectra are such strong

00:03:04.542 --> 00:03:05.644
evidence for Bohr's idea.

00:03:06.407 --> 00:03:08.450
Every element has its own set of energy

00:03:08.491 --> 00:03:10.775
levels, so it gives out its own pattern

00:03:10.796 --> 00:03:12.319
of lines, like a fingerprint.

00:03:13.203 --> 00:03:16.237
Sodium, for example, gives out a strong yellow-orange

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:16.539
line.

00:03:17.042 --> 00:03:18.627
That's the colour you see when a sodium

00:03:18.688 --> 00:03:20.874
compound is held in a flame, and you'll

00:03:20.895 --> 00:03:23.483
collect flame colours yourself in a later practical

00:03:23.524 --> 00:03:23.784
video.

00:03:24.448 --> 00:03:25.914
But Bohr's model had limits.

00:03:26.778 --> 00:03:29.621
It pictured electrons circling the nucleus in neat

00:03:29.822 --> 00:03:30.121
orbits,

00:03:30.623 --> 00:03:33.426
and its sums matched hydrogen's lines very well.

00:03:34.369 --> 00:03:36.595
For atoms with more than one electron, it

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:37.016
didn't work.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.083
In nineteen twenty-six,

00:03:39.606 --> 00:03:42.376
Erwin Schrödinger's model replaced the neat orbits.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.203
It keeps the energy levels, but it doesn't

00:03:45.264 --> 00:03:47.108
track an electron along a path.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:50.077
Instead, it describes orbitals.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:53.485
An orbital is a region around the nucleus

00:03:53.586 --> 00:03:55.469
where the electron is likely to be found.

00:03:56.212 --> 00:03:58.797
Picture a cloud that's densest where the electron

00:03:58.878 --> 00:03:59.999
is most likely to be.

00:04:00.842 --> 00:04:02.003
Inside the energy levels,

00:04:02.525 --> 00:04:05.568
orbitals come in types called s, p, d

00:04:05.989 --> 00:04:06.389
and f,

00:04:06.991 --> 00:04:08.291
which we'll use in a later video.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.376
So here's the picture to keep.

00:04:10.923 --> 00:04:12.299
Stairs, not a ramp.

00:04:13.043 --> 00:04:15.929
Electrons can only change energy in fixed steps,

00:04:16.331 --> 00:04:18.896
so atoms give out only certain photon energies,

00:04:19.378 --> 00:04:20.399
and only certain colours.

00:04:23.032 --> 00:04:23.459
Your turn.

00:04:24.309 --> 00:04:26.352
Two energy levels in an atom are one

00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:28.676
point six times ten to the minus nineteen

00:04:28.717 --> 00:04:29.037
joules,

00:04:29.579 --> 00:04:31.501
and four point one times ten to the

00:04:31.543 --> 00:04:34.066
minus nineteen joules, above the ground state.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:36.683
An electron moves from the higher level to

00:04:36.704 --> 00:04:37.387
the lower level.

00:04:38.072 --> 00:04:40.418
State whether a photon is absorbed or emitted,

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.343
and calculate its energy.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:43.761
Pause here to answer.

00:04:54.475 --> 00:04:57.969
The electron drops, so the atom loses energy,

00:04:58.392 --> 00:04:59.536
and a photon is emitted.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.484
Its energy is the gap: four point one

00:05:02.745 --> 00:05:05.229
minus one point six, which is two point

00:05:05.291 --> 00:05:07.695
five times ten to the minus nineteen joules.

00:05:08.479 --> 00:05:09.142
Adding the two, to

00:05:09.646 --> 00:05:11.657
get five point seven, is the slip.

00:05:12.261 --> 00:05:14.288
A photon carries the difference between the levels,

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:15.031
not the sum.

00:05:16.818 --> 00:05:17.263
Last one.

00:05:18.115 --> 00:05:21.217
An atom has three energy levels, at zero,

00:05:21.579 --> 00:05:23.399
two and five units of energy.

00:05:24.181 --> 00:05:26.643
Which photon energies can it emit, when an

00:05:26.704 --> 00:05:29.126
electron drops from one level to a lower

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:29.327
one?

00:05:30.111 --> 00:05:32.162
Explain why it can't emit a photon with

00:05:32.344 --> 00:05:33.369
four units of energy.

00:05:34.138 --> 00:05:34.968
Pause here to answer.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.465
The possible drops are five to two, two

00:05:47.966 --> 00:05:49.830
to zero, and five to zero.

00:05:50.411 --> 00:05:53.519
So the photon energies are three, two and

00:05:53.599 --> 00:05:54.241
five units.

00:05:55.264 --> 00:05:57.370
Four isn't the gap between any pair of

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.818
levels, so no single drop can make it,

00:06:00.179 --> 00:06:01.884
and there's no line at four units.

00:06:02.848 --> 00:06:05.194
Listing only two and five is the classic

00:06:05.215 --> 00:06:05.474
slip.

00:06:06.037 --> 00:06:07.743
Don't forget the drop between the two upper

00:06:07.784 --> 00:06:08.085
levels.

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:12.169
Notice how the model of the atom kept

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:12.610
changing.

00:06:13.212 --> 00:06:16.037
The nuclear model couldn't explain line spectra, so

00:06:16.117 --> 00:06:16.978
Bohr changed it,

00:06:17.579 --> 00:06:19.943
and Schrödinger changed it again when Bohr's model

00:06:20.023 --> 00:06:21.245
didn't work for bigger atoms.

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.576
Each time, new evidence forced a better model.

00:06:25.378 --> 00:06:27.182
Next time, we'll use that same habit in

00:06:27.262 --> 00:06:28.304
our own investigations,

00:06:28.845 --> 00:06:30.989
and see how new evidence can improve a

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.472
testable chemistry question.

00:06:35.195 --> 00:06:36.017
Here are the key notes.

00:06:36.541 --> 00:06:38.088
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:38.129 --> 00:06:38.550
to copy them.
